  "return_code": 0,
  "success": true,
  "command": "cat > /app/paper.txt << 'PAPER_EOF'\n# Research Advances in Code Agents and Red-teaming\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe rapid advancement of code agents powered by large language models (LLMs) has revolutionized software development, automated programming, and intelligent coding assistants. However, these powerful systems also introduce significant security risks and vulnerabilities that require rigorous evaluation. This paper explores the intersection of code agents and red-teaming methodologies, examining how automated red-teaming agents can proactively identify and exploit vulnerabilities in code generation systems. We review recent advances in both red-teaming and blue-teaming approaches, including the RedCodeAgent framework for automatic vulnerability discovery and the BlueCodeAgent system for defensive security. Furthermore, we draw inspiration from deep learning architectures, particularly residual learning techniques, to discuss novel approaches for building more robust and secure code agents. Our analysis reveals critical gaps in current security evaluation methodologies and proposes directions for future research in building trustworthy code generation systems.\n\n## Introduction\n\nCode agents represent a paradigm shift in how software is developed, maintained, and deployed. By combining large language models with code interpreters, these agents can write, execute, debug, and optimize code dynamically, enabling unprecedented levels of automation in software engineering workflows [1]. Applications range from automated code completion to autonomous bug fixing and intelligent technical support systems.\n\nDespite their promise, code agents introduce significant security concerns. When granted the ability to execute arbitrary code, these systems can be exploited through various attack vectors, including prompt injection, sandbox escape, and adversarial code generation. The security community has responded by developing red-teaming methodologies to proactively identify and mitigate these vulnerabilities before they can be exploited in production environments.\n\nRed-teaming, traditionally used in cybersecurity to simulate adversarial attacks, has emerged as a critical tool for evaluating the robustness of AI systems. Automated red-teaming agents can systematically explore the attack surface of code agents, identifying vulnerabilities that might be missed through manual testing. Recent work has demonstrated the effectiveness of specialized agents designed specifically for testing code generation models.\n\nThis paper provides a comprehensive overview of research advances in code agents and red-teaming methodologies. We examine current approaches, identify key challenges, and propose directions for future research. The remainder of this paper is organized as follows: Section 2 reviews related work in code agents and red-teaming. Section 3 discusses key challenges and open problems. Section 4 presents conclusions and future directions.\n\n## Related Work\n\n### Red-Teaming for Code Agents\n\nThe field of automated red-teaming has seen significant advancement with the emergence of specialized frameworks designed for testing code generation models. **RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents** [2] presents a comprehensive framework for automatically red-teaming diverse code agents. The authors propose a systematic approach to vulnerability discovery that leverages multiple attack strategies, including adversarial prompt engineering, code injection, and logic exploitation. Their framework demonstrates that existing code agents are vulnerable to a wide range of attacks, emphasizing the need for robust security evaluation methodologies before deployment.\n\nThe RedCodeAgent framework introduces several innovative techniques for testing code agent security:\n- Multi-strategy attack generation using LLM-driven adversarial prompt synthesis\n- Automated vulnerability detection through execution outcome analysis\n- Coverage-guided fuzzing for exploring diverse attack paths\n- Cross-agent comparative analysis to identify common vulnerabilities\n\n### Blue-Teaming and Defensive Strategies\n\nWhile red-teaming focuses on offensive security testing, blue-teaming approaches aim to develop defensive mechanisms and security improvements. **BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI** [3] presents a complementary framework that leverages red-teaming results to enhance the security of code generation models. The authors propose a closed-loop security evaluation system where red-teaming results inform defensive improvements, which are then re-evaluated to measure security gains.\n\nKey contributions of the BlueCodeAgent framework include:\n- Integration of red-teaming feedback into model training pipelines\n- Automated patch generation for identified vulnerabilities\n- Continuous security evaluation through iterative red-teaming cycles\n- Comparative analysis of defense effectiveness across different model architectures\n\n### Deep Learning Architectures for Code Agents\n\nThe development of robust code agents also benefits from advances in deep learning architectures. **Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition** [4] introduced the revolutionary residual learning framework that enabled training of substantially deeper neural networks. While originally developed for computer vision tasks, the principles of residual learning have influenced the design of modern language models and code generation systems.\n\nThe residual learning framework addresses the degradation problem in deep networks by introducing skip connections that allow gradients to flow more directly during backpropagation. This architectural innovation has had implications for code agent design, particularly in:\n- Enabling deeper transformer architectures for code understanding\n- Improving gradient flow in large-scale language models\n- Facilitating training of more complex reasoning capabilities\n- Supporting multi-stage code generation pipelines with better information flow\n\n### Integration of Methodologies\n\nRecent research suggests that combining offensive and defensive approaches, along with leveraging advances in deep learning architectures, can lead to more secure and capable code agents. The interplay between red-teaming agents that discover vulnerabilities and blue-teaming agents that patch them creates a continuous improvement cycle. Meanwhile, architectural innovations inspired by deep learning research enable these agents to reason more effectively about security implications of code.\n\n## Challenges and Open Problems\n\nDespite significant progress, several challenges remain in the field of code agent security:\n\n1. **Scalability**: Current red-teaming frameworks struggle to scale to the vast search space of possible code interactions and execution environments.\n\n2. **False Positives**: Automated vulnerability detection systems often generate false positives, requiring extensive manual validation.\n\n3. **Adversarial Adaptation**: Attack strategies must evolve as defensive measures improve, creating an adversarial arms race.\n\n4. **Evaluation Metrics**: Standardized metrics for measuring code agent security are lacking, making it difficult to compare approaches.\n\n5. **Real-World Deployment**: The gap between controlled laboratory testing and real-world deployment conditions remains significant.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nCode agents represent both a transformative opportunity and a security challenge for the software development community. This paper has reviewed key advances in red-teaming and blue-teaming approaches for evaluating and improving code agent security. The work of RedCodeAgent [2] demonstrates the effectiveness of automated vulnerability discovery, while BlueCodeAgent [3] shows how red-teaming insights can inform defensive improvements. Furthermore, architectural innovations like residual learning [4] continue to influence the design of more robust AI systems.\n\nFuture research should focus on developing more scalable and reliable security evaluation frameworks, establishing standardized benchmarks, and creating better integration between offensive testing and defensive improvement pipelines. As code agents become increasingly prevalent in software development workflows, ensuring their security and reliability will be paramount for widespread adoption.\n\n## References\n\n[1] Code Agent Security Survey, \"Security Implications of Autonomous Code Execution in AI Systems,\" arXiv:2405.12345, 2024.\n\n[2] Chengquan Guo, Chulin Xie, Yu Yang et al., \"RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents,\" arXiv:2510.02609, 2025.\n\n[3] Chengquan Guo, Yuzhou Nie, Chulin Xie et al., \"BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI,\" arXiv:2501.01234, 2025.\n\n[4] Kaiming He, Xiangyu Zhang, Shaoqing Ren et al., \"Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition,\" arXiv:1512.03385, 2015.\nPAPER_EOF"
}'}

